Built-in Pipes – NestJS (Notes)

1. What is a Pipe in NestJS?
A pipe is used to transform and validate data
Pipes run before the controller method
Commonly used for:
Input validation
Type conversion

2. Why use Pipes?
Ensures clean and valid data
Reduces manual validation code
Improves application reliability

3. Built-in Pipes in NestJS
NestJS provides several built-in pipes:

4. Common Built-in Pipes
1. ValidationPipe
Validates request data using class-validator
Transforms input types
app.useGlobalPipes(new ValidationPipe());

@Body() createUserDto: CreateUserDto


2. ParseIntPipe
Converts string to number
Throws error if conversion fails
@Get(':id')
getUser(@Param('id', ParseIntPipe) id: number) {}


3. ParseFloatPipe
Converts string to float
@Param('price', ParseFloatPipe) price: number


4. ParseBoolPipe
Converts string to boolean
@Query('active', ParseBoolPipe) active: boolean


5. ParseArrayPipe
Converts input into an array
@Query('ids', new ParseArrayPipe({ items: Number }))
ids: number[]


6. ParseUUIDPipe
Validates UUID format
@Param('id', ParseUUIDPipe) id: string


7. DefaultValuePipe
Sets default value if input is undefined
@Query('page', new DefaultValuePipe(1), ParseIntPipe) page: number


5. Where Pipes Can Be Applied
Level
Example

Global
app.useGlobalPipes()

Controller
@UsePipes()

Route
@Param(), @Body()


6. Pipe Execution Flow
Request → Pipe → Controller → Service


7. Key Points (Exam Ready)
Pipes transform & validate data
Run before controller logic
Built-in pipes reduce boilerplate
Can be global, controller, or method-level

8. One-Line Definition
Built-in pipes in NestJS are used to automatically transform and validate incoming request data before it reaches the controller.

